ear engaged but not disruptive. There is no visual cue of a player being distracted by external factors.\n\n**4) Scoreboard Showing Early Game Scores**\n\n*   **Yes, the scoreboard is visible and shows early game scores.** In multiple frames (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12), the scoreboard is displayed in the lower-left corner. It consistently reads:\n    *   **DJOKOVIC 1**\n    *   **TSITSIPAS 0**\n    *   The point score is also shown as **0 - 0** or **0 - 15** (in Frame 12), indicating the very beginning of a game. This confirms the video captures the start of the match, specifically the first game of the first set.\n\n**5) Any Visual Evidence that Would Explain Why the Start Was Considered \'Awful\'**\n\n*   **The most compelling visual evidence is in Frame 14.** The image of the tennis ball lying idle on the court, far from any player, after a point has clearly concluded, suggests a point was lost quickly and perhaps unceremoniously. This could be interpreted as an "awful" start if it was a result of a poor shot, a double fault, or a simple unforced error by one of the players, leading to a swift point loss. The fact that the scoreboard shows Djokovic leading 1-0 (with the point score at 0-15 in Frame 12) implies Tsitsipas is serving and losing points early. The ball\'s position in Frame 14 is the strongest visual indicator of a poor, ineffective shot that likely contributed to a quick point loss, which could be described as an "awful" start to the match for the player who made the error.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nWhile there is no direct visual proof of a service error or a ball hit into the net/out of bounds, **Frame 14 provides the strongest visual evidence** that could explain why the start of the match might be considered "awful." The image of the ball lying still on the court suggests a point was lost due to a poor or ineffective shot, likely an unforced error, which would be a disappointing way to begin a high-stakes match. The scoreboard confirms